      "statement": "The verb raises from V to I and adjoins to the inflectional head, forming the complex verb okumuş.",
      "stageRecord": "The suffix -muş is a bound affix and cannot stand alone, so the verb stem must combine with it. The verb oku- undergoes head movement out of V-bar and left-adjoins to I. The stem comes first because the suffix follows it. The adjunction creates a complex I node, and the original I remains its lower segment. A coindexed trace stays in the verb's base position, to the right of the object. Two relations are established, in this order. First, V-to-I head movement links the raised verb to its trace and to the complex I landing site; the raised verb antecedent-governs the trace. Second, the complex I head is formed as one morphological word, so the stem and suffix together are spelled out as okumuş. This second relation establishes that the complex head realizes the text 'okumuş'.",
